WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.442
A human-machine interface, or HMI, is the operator's window into an industrial process.

00:00:06.442 --> 00:00:16.439
It may be a panel mounted near equipment, a workstation in a control room, or part of a larger supervisory platform.

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:24.214
The HMI translates controller data into graphics, values, trends, alarms, and controls that people can use.

00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:27.990
Process visibility must be timely and trustworthy.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:34.433
Operators rely on displays to understand equipment state, production conditions, and developing problems.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:43.096
Alarm functions call attention to conditions that require evaluation, while acknowledgment and history help teams manage the response.

00:00:43.096 --> 00:00:49.983
Many HMIs also permit manual commands, setpoint changes, mode selection, and other authorized actions.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:54.648
This makes an HMI more than a passive display.

00:00:54.648 --> 00:01:00.201
Its permissions, application configuration, and communication paths can directly influence operations.

00:01:00.201 --> 00:01:03.978
Compromise or misuse can have several effects.

00:01:03.978 --> 00:01:08.643
False or stale information may conceal a real problem.

00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:11.975
An inappropriate command may alter equipment.

00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:19.306
Loss of the interface can leave operators with limited visibility even if controllers continue running.

00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:24.860
A flood of meaningless alarms can distract from conditions that matter.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:35.300
Protection includes managed user accounts, least privilege, controlled remote access, application and configuration change control, tested updates, secure backups, and audit records.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:41.743
HMI systems should not be used for general browsing or routine office work.

00:01:41.743 --> 00:01:48.185
Redundant consoles and manual operating procedures may support resilience when a workstation fails.

00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:53.294
During investigation, distinguish the displayed view from the underlying process.

00:01:53.294 --> 00:01:59.736
Compare HMI evidence with controller status, historian trends, field observations, and operator reports.

00:01:59.736 --> 00:02:07.067
The best security response restores trustworthy situational awareness without issuing uncoordinated actions to the process.
